1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,421
Lumen's network is ready.

2
00:00:02,371 --> 00:00:03,850
Let's remove the wait.

3
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:08,720
Lumen provides Internet-on-Demand on a network controlled by software.

4
00:00:08,670 --> 00:00:12,601
In buildings already connected to Lumen, the fiber is in place.

5
00:00:12,601 --> 00:00:16,465
An API call is simply an instruction between systems.

6
00:00:16,465 --> 00:00:20,850
One call can activate eligible services, with no new construction cost.

7
00:00:21,250 --> 00:00:26,068
The customer emails an agent, who reads the message to work out what is needed.

8
00:00:26,018 --> 00:00:33,132
The agent checks local services and prices using Lumen's APIs, chooses a cloud provider, and prepares a quote.

9
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:39,320
The agent then retypes the details into JSON, the order message Lumen needs.

10
00:00:39,270 --> 00:00:41,486
This can introduce errors.

11
00:00:41,486 --> 00:00:44,986
Quotes can take one to three weeks, delaying revenue.

12
00:00:46,250 --> 00:00:48,693
Our goal is to remove that delay.

13
00:00:48,643 --> 00:00:52,064
Customers describe their needs in everyday language.

14
00:00:52,064 --> 00:00:56,234
The broker handles checks and orders for existing and new customers.

15
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:00,653
First, the AI reads the customer's request.

16
00:01:00,603 --> 00:01:04,058
The broker checks the site and its available capacity.

17
00:01:04,058 --> 00:01:09,850
It compares cloud options and calculates costs from fixed rates, so the AI never invents a price.

18
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:13,390
The customer reviews the quote and clicks approve.

19
00:01:13,340 --> 00:01:18,850
The broker checks the order details, sends the API request, and returns the result.

20
00:01:19,250 --> 00:01:25,317
We used Lumen's Developer Portal to model how service checks, quotes, and cloud orders should work.

21
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:30,850
This Ordering API sends the approved order with the customer's Lumen identifier.

22
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:33,605
Customers do not write JSON.

23
00:01:33,557 --> 00:01:36,850
The broker fills this order message with their billing account and quote.

24
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:40,503
Our mock APIs simulate these steps safely.

25
00:01:40,453 --> 00:01:43,737
No live connections or real bills are created.

26
00:01:44,250 --> 00:01:48,509
An existing customer requests five gigabits with attack protection.

27
00:01:48,459 --> 00:01:51,850
After one question, they confirm Dallas and Google Cloud.

28
00:01:52,250 --> 00:01:56,751
The price is six dollars fifteen per hour, with no construction cost.

29
00:01:56,701 --> 00:02:00,690
Six unused network ports are already available in Dallas.

30
00:02:01,250 --> 00:02:05,850
The quote lists every charge, including the connection and attack protection.

31
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:09,719
This table explains the cost of each cloud option.

32
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:14,850
The diagram shows how Dallas connects through Lumen's protected network to Google Cloud.

33
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:18,750
The customer approves the quote before anything is ordered.

34
00:02:19,250 --> 00:02:22,762
The broker checks that the order matches the approved quote.

35
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:25,443
The test order succeeds.

36
00:02:25,393 --> 00:02:29,461
In production, billing could start as soon as the connection is active.

37
00:02:30,250 --> 00:02:34,495
A new customer describes Apple's Chicago office with five floors.

38
00:02:34,445 --> 00:02:37,850
They want AWS and protection from online attacks.

39
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:41,336
That description fills the form automatically.

40
00:02:41,286 --> 00:02:45,850
The customer can check and edit every detail without waiting for a discovery call.

41
00:02:46,250 --> 00:02:52,763
After checking available services, it recommends thirty-two megabits and five floor switches for this office.

42
00:02:53,250 --> 00:02:59,796
At about one dollar eight per hour, AWS is the lowest-priced option, with no new construction cost.

43
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:05,850
The final quote shows all charges, so the customer knows the full price before committing.

44
00:03:06,250 --> 00:03:11,445
One click creates the account and places the order, without retyping any details.

45
00:03:12,250 --> 00:03:14,636
Lumen can start earning sooner.

46
00:03:14,586 --> 00:03:20,850
At this example rate, removing three weeks of delay brings forward about three thousand dollars per connection.

47
00:03:21,250 --> 00:03:24,008
First, Lumen earns revenue sooner.

48
00:03:23,958 --> 00:03:29,401
Second, staff spend less time preparing routine quotes, which lowers sales costs.

49
00:03:29,401 --> 00:03:35,128
Third, customers can buy extra capacity more easily, so existing fiber earns more.

50
00:03:35,128 --> 00:03:39,344
Fourth, checked orders mean fewer mistakes and less support work.

51
00:03:39,344 --> 00:03:44,139
Finally, instant prices help more new customers buy before they lose interest.

52
00:03:45,250 --> 00:03:50,850
Next, we plan to connect live Lumen services, pricing, and secure customer accounts.

53
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:56,850
Later, it could adjust capacity automatically and help find, fix, and prevent network problems.

54
00:03:57,250 --> 00:03:58,623
Thank you.
